                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Drop Alive is a cute little platform game where I play as a water drop trying to make my way through a house and eventually reach a nearby river. The idea is simple, but I actually like how much the game builds around being made of water. I can move through the levels, avoid hazards and change between liquid, solid and gas forms depending on what I need to get past. It is the sort of game that looks very harmless at first, but some of the later sections can be surprisingly awkward and difficult.\n\nThe different forms are easily my favourite part. As a liquid I can move normally, while ice lets me deal with certain surfaces and steam gives me a completely different way of moving through the level. I have to pay attention to the environment because water, heat and other objects can change what state I am in. I like that the game does not just give me three forms for the sake of having three forms. They are actually tied into the level design, so I have to think about which state is useful before I move forward.\n\nThere are 12 levels and each one has its own look and hazards, which helps keep the short adventure from feeling completely repetitive. I am dealing with things like jars, pipes, spikes and other obstacles while trying not to lose too much of my water. Collecting the little tokens hidden around the levels also gives me another reason to explore instead of simply rushing for the exit. There are 9 achievements and 12 unlockable skins as well, so I have a few extra things to work towards after finishing the main path.\n\nThe biggest problem for me is the movement. The idea behind the game is good, but some of the controls do not feel as precise as I want them to be. That becomes a bigger issue when I am trying to make careful jumps or move through sections where a small mistake sends me back to the beginning. The steam form can also be a little awkward because the movement feels different from the normal platforming. I do not mind difficult games, but I prefer when I fail because I made a bad move rather than because the controls made a simple action feel uncertain.\n\nThe presentation is probably the easiest thing for me to like. The hand drawn art is colourful and gives the little water drop a lot of charm, while the music and sound effects fit the light hearted atmosphere. I also like how each area has its own style instead of making the whole game look exactly the same. It is not a technically impressive game by modern standards, but I think the artwork has aged better than some of the actual mechanics because it still gives the game a nice personality.\n\nI also think the game is best when I treat it as a short platforming challenge rather than a huge adventure. There is enough here for me to enjoy the different mechanics and search for the collectibles, but I am not getting a massive campaign with lots of different systems. The good thing is that the base game is free, so I do not feel like I am missing out on a huge amount of content for the price. There is also a small Deluxe Edition available with extra content, but I do not need it to understand what the main game is about.\n\nOverall, Drop Alive has a genuinely clever idea underneath its cute presentation. I really like the three water forms, the level variety and the way the environment affects how I move through each stage. The controls are the biggest thing stopping me from rating it higher because some sections become frustrating for reasons that are not entirely my fault. Even so, it is short, free and different enough from a normal platformer that I think it is worth playing. It is a good little game that gets close to being something great, but the rough movement keeps it just below that level for me.\n\nRating: 7.4/10\n",
